    "question": {
      ":question/id": "71f74957-013e-5d43-850d-e16d2473b62a",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 7 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 17 & 19 & 23\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 34 \\\\ 17 & -15 & 74\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the right of $a_{11}=2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A &=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 7 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 17 & 19 & 23\\end{bmatrix}\n\\\\ &\\xrightarrow{C_{2}:=C_{2}-2C_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 13 \\\\ 17 & -15 & 23\\end{bmatrix}\n\\\\ &\\xrightarrow{C_{3}:=C_{3}+3C_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 34 \\\\ 17 & -15 & 74\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$C_{2}:=C_{2}-2C_{1}$ and $C_{3}:=C_{3}+3C_{1}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
